---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const description =
  "Stan Angeloff — hands-on engineer with 20+ years building production systems. AI-agent infrastructure, platform & developer experience. Based in Sofia, Bulgaria.";
---

<BaseLayout title="About" activeNav="about" description={description} ogImage={new URL("/og/default.png", Astro.site).href}>
  <article>
    <h1 class="about-title">About</h1>
    <div class="about-title-rule">
      <span>&sect;</span>
      <span class="line"></span>
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        I'm Stan Angeloff &mdash; a hands-on engineer with 20+ years of
        building production systems. Based in Sofia, Bulgaria. I go deepest
        as a senior individual contributor &mdash; give me a hard problem
        and room to work, not a delivery board to manage. This blog is my
        personal space for thoughts, rants &amp; research.
      </p>

      <h2><span class="accent">&#9670;</span>What I'm up to (2025&mdash;)</h2>
      <p>
        Most of my free time goes into <a
          href="https://github.com/Flemma-Dev/flemma.nvim">Flemma</a
        >
        &mdash; a low-level AI workspace that runs inside Neovim. Not a wrapper, the
        actual thing: streaming APIs, tool execution, prompt pipelines, talking to
        Anthropic, OpenAI and Google directly. I built it because I needed it for
        work &mdash; PRDs, statements of work, R&amp;D spikes, client documentation.
        Every feature exists because a real problem demanded it.
      </p>
      <p>
        The core belief: models come and go, but the workspace you build around
        them is yours. Go one level lower than you think you need to. Write your
        own agents, wire up your own tool execution, parse your own streaming
        responses. The best way to understand AI tooling is to build it
        yourself.
      </p>
      <p>
        By day I'm CTO and hands-on architect at <a
          href="https://www.luup.com/">Luup</a
        > &mdash; a white-label &ldquo;Community Commerce&rdquo; platform.
        The majority of the codebase is mine. I also set up the team's
        AI-agent engineering practices &mdash; review skills, production
        guardrails, the works &mdash; so the whole team can adopt LLMs
        safely.
      </p>
      <p>
        I'm also experimenting with <a
          href="https://github.com/Flemma-Dev/voxize">Voxize</a
        >, a voice-to-text tool for Linux/Wayland &mdash; my daily driver for
        AI-assisted coding instead of reaching for the keyboard.
      </p>

      <h2><span class="accent">&#9670;</span>Technical background</h2>
      <p>
        I've been at this for over two decades, touching just about everything
        along the way. Some highlights from the day job:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Nuclear</strong> &mdash; built an operations platform for the
          Kozloduy nuclear power plant (2004 &mdash; .NET + AJAX before
          &ldquo;AJAX&rdquo; was coined)
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Enterprise B2B</strong> &mdash; Saudi Aramco, the UK Post
          Office, Forever Living (&pound;1M ARR) &mdash; a decade of SaaS
          platforms at <a href="https://psp-webtech.co.uk">PSP</a>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Fraud &amp; fintech</strong> &mdash; real-time
          affiliate-fraud detection at ~1M conversions/day; an immutable,
          double-entry accounting ledger on Postgres
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Migrations</strong> &mdash; replatformed ~50 storefronts to
          Shopify Plus with zero prior experience; reverse-engineered
          ~100,000 lines of Zoho Deluge to retrofit an API over a legacy CRM
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        And from the open-source archives:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>CoffeeScript</strong> &mdash; used to be a top 10 contributor to
          <a
            href="https://github.com/jashkenas/coffeescript/graphs/contributors?all=1"
            >jashkenas/coffeescript</a
          > (now sitting at #9, but who's counting)
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>PHP</strong> &mdash; past maintainer of <a
            href="https://github.com/StanAngeloff/php.vim">php.vim</a
          >, the de-facto Vim syntax for PHP (473+ stars) until Tree-sitter
          superseded it
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>JavaScript</strong> &mdash; built <a
            href="https://github.com/StanAngeloff/zoey">Zoey</a
          >, a lightweight mobile app framework, and <a
            href="https://github.com/StanAngeloff/lotte">Lotte</a
          >, headless browser testing with PhantomJS before Puppeteer existed
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Ruby</strong> &mdash; built <a
            href="https://github.com/StanAngeloff/compass-magick"
            >compass-magick</a
          > and <a href="https://github.com/StanAngeloff/compass-canvas"
            >compass-canvas</a
          >, dynamic image generation in pure Sass
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Shell &amp; terminal</strong> &mdash; a big believer in open, free,
          terminal-based tools. Built <a
            href="https://github.com/StanAngeloff/vagrant-shell-scripts"
            >vagrant-shell-scripts</a
          > back when provisioning VMs was the thing to do
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>GitOps</strong> &mdash; managed all my PCs with <a
            href="https://github.com/StanAngeloff/longitude">Ansible</a
          > for years, even <a
            href="https://github.com/RogueDudes/automate-all-the-things"
            >gave a talk about it</a
          > back when Ansible was the underdog. Now I run <a
            href="https://github.com/StanAngeloff/nix-meridian">NixOS</a
          >
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        TypeScript, Node.js, and whatever else the job demands &mdash; I've
        never been one to stick to a single stack.
      </p>

      <h2><span class="accent">&#9670;</span>The honest bit</h2>
      <p>
        As you can tell, most of those OSS projects are pretty old. I haven't been
        as active in the community as I'd like &mdash; however, I'm still here,
        still tinkering, and Flemma is where that energy goes now.
      </p>

      <p style="overflow-wrap:break-word">
        You can find me on<br class="mobile-break" /> <a href="https://github.com/StanAngeloff">GitHub</a
        ><span style="color:var(--ink-faint);margin:0 .25em">&middot;</span><a
          href="https://news.ycombinator.com/user?id=StanAngeloff"
          >Hacker&nbsp;News</a
        ><span style="color:var(--ink-faint);margin:0 .25em">&middot;</span><a
          href="https://mastodon.social/@stanangeloff"
          rel="me">Mastodon</a
        ><span style="color:var(--ink-faint);margin:0 .25em">&middot;</span><a
          href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/stanangeloff/">LinkedIn</a
        ><span style="color:var(--ink-faint);margin:0 .25em">&middot;</span><a
          href="https://x.com/StanAngeloff">X</a
        >
      </p>

      <div class="about-photo">
        <img
          src="/assets/images/stan-angeloff.jpg"
          alt="Stan Angeloff"
          width="280"
          height="280"
          loading="lazy"
        />
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="end-mark">&sect;</div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
